My verdict

For pure rucking, this is the GORUCK bag I'd pick

The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 is built for one job, carrying a plate, and it does that job better than any other GORUCK bag I've carried. On a filmed September 2026 hike on hilly Wisconsin trail with a 20 lb plate, the 25L stayed locked to my back and the lumbar pad felt supportive. I carried it without the optional hip belt and my traps were sore afterward, so for heavier loads I'd set up the sternum strap and try the belt.

What I liked

Stayed solid against my back over uneven, hilly ground with a 20 lb plate and extra gear

The large lumbar pad felt supportive without demanding attention during the walk

The 25L left room for camera gear alongside the plate; internal pockets were useful for keys

What held it back

My traps felt sore after I got home; I carried it without the optional hip belt

The test

Key findings

I carried the 25L GORUCK Rucker 5.0 on uneven, hilly Wisconsin trail with a 20 lb plate plus camera gear and other supplies I didn't weigh.

The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 stayed stable against my back on rough ground, and I didn't notice the plate shifting inside.

The GORUCK Rucker 5.0's large lumbar pad felt comfortable and supportive, and I soon stopped noticing it while walking.

I carried the GORUCK Rucker 5.0 without its optional hip belt, and my traps were sore once I got home, so for heavier loads I'd add the sternum strap and try the belt.

The GORUCK Rucker 5.0 has two dedicated plate pockets that hold the plate up high against your back instead of letting it sink to the bottom of the bag.

Of the three GORUCK bags I've carried with a 20 lb plate, the Rucker 5.0 is the better pure ruck, because the GR1 and GR2 hold the plate in an open sleeve and have no lumbar pad.

